# RESUME — paste into a fresh {{CAMPAIGN_SLUG}}-PM manager session

## Starting the manager (read this first — everything below is what the manager reads)

The manager is an ordinary Claude Code session whose working directory is this repo. There is
no daemon, no server, nothing to install. Start it like this:

```bash
# 1. Open a tab in the campaign workspace and name it the manager tab.
#    Run `herdr workspace list` to get the workspace id if you don't have it.
herdr tab create --workspace <workspace-id> --label {{CAMPAIGN_SLUG}}-PM

# 2. In that tab, start the session IN THIS REPO:
cd {{WORKTREE_ROOT}}/{{CAMPAIGN_SLUG}}-pm
claude "Read RESUME.md in this directory and resume as the campaign manager."
```

If you are not using herdr, step 2 alone is enough — you lose the automation lane
(`bin/track`'s auto-spawn needs herdr), but every `bin/` tool and the whole human-gated lane
work unchanged.

**Cwd matters.** The `bin/` tools locate this repo by searching upward from `$PWD` for a
`.pm/` directory, so a session started elsewhere will not find the ledger. Start in the repo,
or export `PM_ROOT=<path to this repo>`.

**One manager at a time.** Two manager sessions on the same repo will fight over the event
log — the lock keeps the log valid, but they will make contradictory plans. Workers are the
thing you run many of; the manager is one.

**Ending a session cleanly:** commit and push before you stop. Everything durable is either an
event-log line or a committed file; anything held only in the session's context is lost, and
that is by design — if it mattered, it should have been an event or a report.

